 ## Overview
-This method computes the exact Fourier Transform $\hat{u}_\mathcal{T}$ and its spatial gradients for a single triangle $\mathcal{T}$ defined by vertices $(\mathbf{v}_1, \mathbf{v}_2, \mathbf{v}_3)$ [S1, unofficial]. It enables differentiable rendering by providing analytic derivatives of the basis reconstruction function, avoiding additional samples or ray traversals. It sets itself apart from [Analytic Gradient Computation via Box-Spline Derivatives](3199-analytic-gradient-computation-via-box-spline-derivatives.md) by operating on explicit mesh geometry (triangles) rather than implicit grid-based basis functions, making it suitable for sparse or structured surface representations in volume rendering contexts like fluorescence microscopy [S1, unofficial].
+This method computes the exact Fourier Transform $\hat{u}_\mathcal{T}$ and its spatial gradients for a single triangle $\mathcal{T}$ defined by vertices $(\mathbf{v}_1, \mathbf{v}_2, \mathbf{v}_3)$ [S1, unofficial]. It provides analytic derivatives of the basis reconstruction function, avoiding additional samples or ray traversals [G].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:** Triangle vertices $\mathbf{v}_1, \mathbf{v}_2, \mathbf{v}_3 \in \mathbb{R}^3$; frequency vector $\boldsymbol{\xi} \in \mathbb{R}^3$.
-**Outputs:** Fourier Transform value $\hat{u}_\mathcal{T}(\boldsymbol{\xi})$; Gradient of the transform with respect to vertex positions $\frac{\partial \hat{u}_\mathcal{T}}{\partial \mathbf{v}_p}$.
